e remainder in the form:\n\\[ R(x) = ax + b. \\]\nThis means we can express \\( p(x) \\) as:\n\\[ p(x) = (x-1)(x-3)q(x) + ax + b, \\]\nfor some polynomial \\( q(x) \\).\n\nWe are given two conditions:\n1. When \\( p(x) \\) is divided by \\( x-1 \\), the remainder is 3. This means \\( p(1) = 3 \\).\n2. When \\( p(x) \\) is divided by \\( x-3 \\), the remainder is 5. This means \\( p(3) = 5 \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( x = 1 \\) into the expression for \\( p(x) \\), we get:\n\\[ p(1) = a(1) + b = a + b = 3. \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( x = 3 \\) into the expression for \\( p(x) \\), we get:\n\\[ p(3) = a(3) + b = 3a + b = 5. \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[ \\begin{cases} \na + b = 3 \\\\ \n3a + b = 5 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nTo solve this system, we subtract the first equation from the second:\n\\[ (3a + b) - (a + b) = 5 - 3, \\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[ 2a = 2. \\]\nThus, \\( a = 1 \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( a = 1 \\) back into the first equation:\n\\[ 1 + b = 3, \\]\nwe find:\n\\[ b = 2. \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( p(x) \\) is divided by \\( (x-1)(x-3) \\) is:\n\\[ R(x) = ax + b = 1x + 2 = x + 2. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{x + 2}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{x + 2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:411"}
